## Changed

`npx @micacms/cms` now prints one short banner: the address of the editor, the content directory it reads, and what a save writes. It opens your browser on the editor when you run it from a terminal. Use `--no-open` to stop that.

There is no sign-in screen on your own machine. Local mode has no identity provider, so there is nothing to ask and no account to create. The editor opens directly.

The command also explains the two things that go wrong first. A port that is already in use names the port and gives you the flag to change it. A missing content directory or content model says what the directory is and which command writes the model.

The getting-started guide is five minutes and leads with the local setup.

## Breaking

The default port is **6422**, not 4000. Every other development server reaches for 4000, and Astro's own dev server sits on 4321. If your deployment relies on the default port, republish it and update `CMS_BASE_URL`, the OpenID Connect redirect URI, and any reverse proxy.

**Astro 6 or 7 is required.** Astro 6 moved content-collection schemas to Zod 4, and Mica gives Astro the schemas it derives from your model. The loader contract did not change, so Astro 6 and 7 behave the same. Stay on version 0.0.4 if your project uses Astro 5. Your content is files either way.

**Node 22.12 or newer** is required, which is the floor Astro 6 sets.
